{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1223 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "c6fd7ed135b73c30a5ed197eba5bd171692460ae6013b57b19ff27edf11189f2" :edb/db-before-t 1222 :edb/db-after-t 1223 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534535 50 #inst "2026-10-12T02:16:27.181Z" 13194139534535 17592186045417 true] [17592186119845 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424595 13194139534535 17592186045417 true] [17592186119846 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424594 13194139534535 17592186045417 true] [17592186321062 1071 "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]\\})=$" 13194139534535 17592186045417 true] [17592186321062 1071 "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]})=$" 13194139534535 17592186045417 false] [17592186321062 1074 "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 6 & -10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $1$ pivot column (or alternatively, it has exactly $1$ non-zero row). \n\nTherefore, \n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]\\})=1.$" 13194139534535 17592186045417 true] [17592186321062 1074 "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 6 & -10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $1$ pivot column (or alternatively, it has exactly $1$ non-zero row). \n\nTherefore, \n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]})=1.$" 13194139534535 17592186045417 false] [17592186348199 1071 "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\})=$" 13194139534535 17592186045417 true] [17592186348199 1071 "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]})=$" 13194139534535 17592186045417 false] [17592186348199 1074 "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore,\n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\})=2.$" 13194139534535 17592186045417 true] [17592186348199 1074 "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore,\n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]})=2.$" 13194139534535 17592186045417 false] [17592186348205 1071 "Let ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ be linearly independent vectors, and suppose that the vector $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}+{v}_{4}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ form a basis of $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$\n- $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}})=4$\n- $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4},w})=5$" 13194139534535 17592186045417 false] [17592186348205 1071 "Let ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ be linearly independent vectors, and suppose that the vector $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}+{v}_{4}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ form a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}\\}$\n\nII. $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}\\})=4$\n\nIII. $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4},w\\})=5$" 13194139534535 17592186045417 true] [17592186348205 1074 "Recall that a basis of a vector space $V$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that span $V.$ The dimension of $V$ is the number of vectors in any basis of $V$ (if such a basis exists). \n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}\\}$ is spanned by the vectors by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}.$ Moreover, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ is a basis of the span.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}\\}$ is spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$, and these are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ is a basis of the span. Therefore, we have $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}\\})=4.$\n\n- Statement III is false. We are told that $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}+{v}_{4}.$ Hence,$w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}.$ this means that $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4},w\\}$ is completely spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ only. So, $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4},w\\})=4$ (not $5$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139534535 17592186045417 true] [17592186348205 1074 "Recall that a basis of a vector space $V$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that span $V.$ The dimension of $V$ is the number of vectors in any basis of $V$ (if such a basis exists). \n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is spanned by the vectors by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}.$ Moreover, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ is a basis of the span.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$, and these are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ is a basis of the span. Therefore, we have $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}})=4.$\n\n- Statement III is false. We are told that $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}+{v}_{4}.$ Hence,$w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}.$ this means that $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4},w}$ is completely spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ only. So, $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4},w})=4$ (not $5$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139534535 17592186045417 false] [17592186424581 1043 #edb/ref 17592186424596 13194139534535 17592186045417 true] [17592186424581 1043 #edb/ref 17592186424597 13194139534535 17592186045417 true] [17592186424581 1043 #edb/ref 17592186424598 13194139534535 17592186045417 true] [17592186424581 1043 #edb/ref 17592186424599 13194139534535 17592186045417 true] [17592186424581 1043 #edb/ref 17592186424600 13194139534535 17592186045417 true] [17592186424581 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534535 17592186045417 true] [17592186424581 1045 "The Dimension of a Span" 13194139534535 17592186045417 true] [17592186424581 1046 #edb/ref 17592186424598 13194139534535 17592186045417 true] [17592186424581 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046659 13194139534535 17592186045417 true] [17592186424581 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048218 13194139534535 17592186045417 true] [17592186424581 1053 #uuid "948a33eb-6d1f-58c5-9917-674dc68468c6" 13194139534535 17592186045417 true] [17592186424582 1080 "3" 13194139534535 17592186045417 true] [17592186424582 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534535 17592186045417 true] [17592186424582 1083 #uuid "3a113c4d-a0ac-5651-906e-41d84f5d836e" 13194139534535 17592186045417 true] [17592186424583 1080 "6" 13194139534535 17592186045417 true] [17592186424583 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534535 17592186045417 true] [17592186424583 1083 #uuid "5368a6fd-1c3c-5ec5-98c7-f3629d82df88" 13194139534535 17592186045417 true] [17592186424584 1080 "0" 13194139534535 17592186045417 true] [17592186424584 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534535 17592186045417 true] [17592186424584 1083 #uuid "73c49f7c-a1f3-56e9-95d3-4e8d7959ede8" 13194139534535 17592186045417 true] [17592186424585 1080 "0" 13194139534535 17592186045417 true] [17592186424585 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534535 17592186045417 true] [17592186424585 1083 #uuid "